<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-laplace-derivatives" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Laplace Transforms of Derivatives</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTRO
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Our goal for this chapter is to use the Laplace transform to solve second-order linear IVPs of the form
    <me>ax''(t) + bx'(t) + cx(t) = g(t).</me>
    To do this we need to know how the Laplace transform interacts with derivatives.
    Under mild conditions on <m>x(t)</m> (essentially that <m>\mathcal{L}\{x\}</m> is defined),
    we have the following two formulas.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       L{x'} WITH JUSTIFICATION DROPDOWN
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Writing <m>X(s) = \mathcal{L}\{x(t)\}</m>:
    <me>\mathcal{L}\{x'(t)\} = sX(s) - x(0).</me>
  </p>

  <justification>
    <title>Justification (can skim)</title>
    <p>
      Starting from the definition and using integration by parts with
      <m>u = e^{-st}</m> and <m>dv = x'(t)\,dt</m>,
      so <m>du = -se^{-st}\,dt</m> and <m>v = x(t)</m>:
      <md>
        <mrow>\mathcal{L}\{x'(t)\}
          \amp= \int_0^{\infty} e^{-st} x'(t)\,dt</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \Bigl[x(t)e^{-st}\Bigr]_0^{\infty} + s\int_0^{\infty} e^{-st} x(t)\,dt</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \Bigl(\lim_{t\to\infty} x(t)e^{-st} - x(0)e^{0}\Bigr) + s\,\mathcal{L}\{x(t)\}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= 0 - x(0) + s\,X(s).</mrow>
      </md>
      The limit <m>\lim_{t\to\infty} x(t)e^{-st} = 0</m> whenever <m>\mathcal{L}\{x\}</m> is defined.
    </p>
  </justification>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       L{x''} WITH JUSTIFICATION DROPDOWN
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    <me>\mathcal{L}\{x''(t)\} = s^2 X(s) - sx(0) - x'(0).</me>
  </p>

  <justification>
    <title>Justification (can skim)</title>
    <p>
      Apply the result for <m>\mathcal{L}\{x'\}</m> twice.
      Let <m>f = x'</m>, so <m>f' = x''</m> and <m>f(0) = x'(0)</m>:
      <md>
        <mrow>\mathcal{L}\{x''(t)\}
          \amp= \mathcal{L}\{f'(t)\}
           = s\,\mathcal{L}\{f(t)\} - f(0)</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= s\,\mathcal{L}\{x'(t)\} - x'(0)</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= s\bigl(sX(s) - x(0)\bigr) - x'(0)</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= s^2 X(s) - sx(0) - x'(0).</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
  </justification>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       THEOREM: RESTATE RESULTS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    We restate these results in the following theorem for easy reference.
  </p>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-laplace-derivatives">
    <title>Laplace Transforms of Derivatives</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>X(s) = \mathcal{L}\{x(t)\}</m>. Then:
        <md>
          <mrow>\mathcal{L}\{x'(t)\} \amp= sX(s) - x(0)</mrow>
          <mrow>\mathcal{L}\{x''(t)\} \amp= s^2 X(s) - sx(0) - x'(0).</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <p>
    Notice what these formulas do: they replace derivatives of <m>x(t)</m> with algebraic
    expressions in <m>s</m>, incorporating the initial conditions <m>x(0)</m> and <m>x'(0)</m>
    automatically. This is the key reason the Laplace transform is so useful for IVPs.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       THREE-STEP STRATEGY
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    With these formulas in hand, here is the strategy for solving an IVP using the Laplace transform:
  </p>

  <p>
    <ol>
      <li><p>
        <alert>Transform.</alert> Apply <m>\mathcal{L}</m> to both sides of the ODE.
        Use the formulas above to replace <m>x'</m> and <m>x''</m> with expressions in <m>X(s)</m>,
        plugging in the initial conditions as you go.
        This turns the ODE into an algebraic equation in <m>s</m>.
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <alert>Solve for <m>X(s)</m>.</alert> Solve the algebraic equation to find <m>X(s)</m>.
        This usually involves factoring and partial fraction decomposition.
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <alert>Invert.</alert> Apply the inverse Laplace transform <m>\mathcal{L}^{-1}</m>
        to <m>X(s)</m> to recover the solution <m>x(t)</m>.
      </p></li>
    </ol>
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <m>\mathcal{L}\{x'\} = sX(s) - x(0)</m> and
          <m>\mathcal{L}\{x''\} = s^2X(s) - sx(0) - x'(0)</m>.
          Initial conditions are built right into these formulas.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          Taking the Laplace transform of an ODE turns it into an algebraic equation in <m>s</m>,
          which is generally much easier to solve.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          The three steps are: transform, solve for <m>X(s)</m>, then invert to get <m>x(t)</m>.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

